# The tests ubhost fails as a drop-in, by design, and why. ubhost is the
# library's test host (tools/ubhost.c): run in place of the shell, its
# runs keep to the library's contract, so what needs a process of the
# shell's own, a terminal or job control, or the executable's signal
# channel differs. Anything failing beyond this list is a bug.
#
# POSIX, on Linux (the oracle image, 8 at a time):
#   ls t/exec/*.sh t/posix/*.sh |
#   xargs -P 8 -n 4 env RECORD_SHELL=$PWD/build-lin/ubhost RECORD_CHECK=1 tools/record
# Editor sessions, on Linux (tools/edrecord's container, with a Linux
# ubhost built for its image): ubhost leads the pty's session there, so
# the run has UB_TERMINAL, as Prospero's terminal tab:
#   EDRECORD_SHELL=$PWD/build-lin/ubhost EDRECORD_CHECK=1 tools/edrecord t/edit/*.ed
# Windows (t/win32/run, WORKDIR outside %TEMP%):
#   t/win32/run -s $PWD/ubhost.exe -x WORKDIR      (ubhost32.exe beside it)
#   t/win32/run -s $PWD/ubhost32.exe WORKDIR       (Windows XP)
# This is free and unencumbered software released into the public domain.

# Job control and terminals: a run has no process groups and no stopped
# jobs (set -m is inert or refused; suspend is refused), and no terminal
# but under UB_TERMINAL, which ubhost has leading a terminal's session
# (or with UBHOST_TERM=1)
t/exec/905-stopped-jobs.sh          stopped jobs (times out: nothing stops)
t/exec/906-stopped-pipelines.sh     stopped pipelines
t/exec/909-interactive-jobs.sh      interactive job reports need a terminal
t/exec/944-stopped-shell-stages.sh  shell code stopped with its job
t/win32/008-interactive.sh          Ctrl-C to an interactive shell's prompt
t/win32/019-interactive-jobs.sh     interactive job control on a console
t/win32/022-fg-job.sh               Ctrl-C to a job fg brought forward

# Terminal mode is bash +m on the terminal (notes/library.md): bash's
# screens are recorded under job control (-m), where the terminal's ^C
# reaches only the foreground job and an ended job is reported at the
# next prompt. Under +m the shell takes the terminal's ^C itself, and
# drops ended jobs unreported: with "keys set +m" first, 058, 059 and
# 146 match bash's screens. Under +m bash drops a ^C that comes as a
# foreground program exits normally, and so does a run, whose wait polls
# the program's exit where bash's takes SIGCHLD: a rare race in both.
t/edit/057-jobctl.ed                ^Z stops nothing, no jobs/fg/bg of stopped jobs
t/edit/058-wait-intr.ed             no "Done" report at the prompt (+m)
t/edit/059-intr-subshell.ed         the shell's INT trap runs for the job's ^C (+m)
t/edit/146-pipe-intr.ed             the shell's INT trap runs, the loop goes on (+m)
# Hangups: leftovers are in the host's group, the terminal's foreground
# group, so the kernel hangs them up as the host exits; bash's jobs were
# in groups of their own
t/edit/150-hangup-disown-h.ed       disown -h changes nothing: the kernel's HUP
t/edit/153-hangup-source.ed         sleep & gets the kernel's HUP
# A background level is a fiber of the run ($! is a virtual pid, as for
# t/exec/877), and a nested ubhost, not its session's leader, has no
# terminal mode, so its run's leftovers are killed as it ends
t/edit/149-hangup-nohup.ed          $! is not a process (nohup's sleep does survive)
t/edit/155-hangup-foreground.ed     the job's report shows a virtual $!
t/edit/156-hangup-ignored.ed        the nested run kills its leftover, HUP ignored or not
# Windows native mode, failing on every POSIX build, the executable's too
t/edit/082-native-drive.ed          native mode is Windows only
t/edit/086-native-prompt.ed         native mode is Windows only
t/edit/087-native-busybox.ed        native mode is Windows only
t/edit/089-native-ctrl-z.ed         native mode is Windows only

# Images: a background level is a fiber of the run, not a process ($!
# is a virtual pid, as under tests --fibers)
t/exec/877-bg-process.sh            $! is not a process
t/win32/016-bg-image.sh             no image self-spawn: leftovers killed

# exec does not replace the host: the program runs while the run waits,
# so the pid is the shell's and a signal to it reaches the shell
t/exec/880-exec-forks.sh            exec with procsubs and jobs in flight
t/posix/001-processes.sh            exec keeps the pid; another process's level
t/posix/003-signals.sh              QUIT and INT to an exec'd shell's pid

# Signals: a subshell is a level, and a stop signal it sends to $$ is
# refused (the host's pid), where bash's parent process runs its trap
t/exec/955-trap-stop-self.sh        ( kill -TTOU $$ ) under a parent's trap
t/win32/017-signals.sh              no signal channel between shells
t/win32/012-ctrlc-young.sh          no Ctrl-C judging (Windows 7 and later; XP when the Ctrl-C wins the race)

# Flaky under load: a resize's WINCH reaches the run through ubhost's
# signal thread, after the keys ptyrun writes right behind it at times
# (the executable takes it on the reading thread), so the trap's
# history -d has not run when the key reads the history (097), or the
# key is read before the trap runs (099)
t/edit/097-history-shrinks.ed       a key handled before the resize's WINCH trap
t/edit/099-nested-read.ed           a key read before the resize's WINCH trap

# A closed std descriptor: the contract has none (ub_cmd.std -1 is the
# null device, and one not open is the host's error), so ubhost passes
# the null device for it, where bash finds it closed (EBADF)
t/exec/1056-start-no-stdin.sh       fd 0 closed: reads see end of file, dups work
t/exec/1057-start-no-stdout.sh      fd 1 or 2 closed: writes and dups succeed
t/exec/1058-start-no-input.sh       commands read from a closed fd 0 are none (0, not 126)
t/posix/013-closed-std-in-job.sh    a job's dup of a closed fd 1 or 2 succeeds

# The working directory is held by name: the library never moves the
# host's cwd and pins no directory (plt_cwd), so a removed one is lost
# to programs, and a moved one to the run's relative names and pwd -P
t/posix/004-gone-cwd.sh             programs refused in a removed directory
t/posix/005-background-children.sh  likewise, from a background job
t/posix/009-unnamed-start-dir.sh    a nested shell refused in a removed directory, and by name in the linked one (ELOOP)
t/posix/018-lost-directory.sh       moved, removed or relinked: pwd -P and cd fail

# Limits: the run never sets the process's (ulimit keeps a table, as a
# subshell's, for its programs), and its descriptor budget is the
# host's process's, beside the host's own and the run's copies of std
t/exec/1031-glob-deep-pattern.sh    ulimit -s 2048 does not shrink the run's stack: no 139
t/posix/007-few-descriptors.sh      under ulimit -n 12 the nested host has no room for a pipe
t/posix/010-write-errors.sh         ulimit -f 1 does not limit the run's own writes

# A background level is a fiber of the run's one thread, and programs'
# exits are held as zombies until the run ends (their groups never
# another's)
t/exec/629-kill-blocked.sh          kill -0 finds the nested shell's held zombie: gone 0
t/exec/1111-signal-comsub-wait.sh   a job's trapped kill of $$ lands after the job ends: wait gives 0, not 138 (tests --fibers too)
t/posix/012-job-fifo-writes.sh      the job's open of a FIFO with no reader blocks the run (times out)

# Flaky for the executable too, not the library's
t/exec/931-setlocale-programs.sh    fails only under the parallel run's load
t/exec/1113-subshell-start-line.sh  a stage's stderr against cat's output of its stdout, merged
t/exec/892-ulimit.sh                the oracle container's soft core limit is now 0 (recorded unlimited)
t/win32/015-fds.sh                  XP: busybox diff's stat of a procsub bridge
